WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.805 --> 00:00:12.561
BEN: Hello, Little Fox readers! Welcome back to People in the News . Today Olivia is going to tell

2
00:00:12.562 --> 00:00:21.888
us about a very important world figure: Ban Ki-moon, the eighth secretary-general of the United Nations. The first secretary-general

3
00:00:21.889 --> 00:00:29.546
described this position as the most impossible job on earth. Isn't that right, Olivia? OLIVIA: That's right, Ben. But

4
00:00:29.547 --> 00:00:37.109
luckily Ban's disposition and some of his life experiences have helped to prepare him for it. Ban was born on

5
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:43.847
June 13, 1944, in a small farm village in South Korea. When the Korean War broke out in 1950,

6
00:00:43.848 --> 00:00:50.599
Ban's family fled to the mountains to escape the fighting. Even though Ban and his family were safe, they

7
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:59.514
were poor and hungry, and they often still heard and saw signs of the war. Ban also saw Americans

8
00:00:59.515 --> 00:01:07.252
for the first time—soldiers who tossed gum, biscuits, and chocolates to Korean children. Growing up during the war, Ban

9
00:01:07.253 --> 00:01:13.998
witnessed many of the hardships brought on by political instability. But during the war, and afterward, Ban also witnessed

10
00:01:13.999 --> 00:01:24.747
the United Nations helping to rebuild Korea, with many countries working together to solve problems. This planted a seed

11
00:01:24.748 --> 00:01:31.079
inside him, encouraging him to think about a career in public service one day. Ban was a diligent student in

12
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:38.985
school, working especially hard to master the English he'd first heard from American soldiers. According to his mother, he

13
00:01:38.986 --> 00:01:49.370
often walked about six kilometers to a fertilizer plant, just for the chance to practice English with the Americans

14
00:01:49.371 --> 00:01:57.377
working there. Ban's diligence paid off in 1962 when he won an English competition sponsored by the American Red

15
00:01:57.378 --> 00:02:05.784
Cross. The prize was a trip to the United States, where Ban had the opportunity to meet President John

16
00:02:05.785 --> 00:02:10.876
F. Kennedy at the White House. Ban later said that the trip also inspired him to become a diplomat

17
00:02:10.877 --> 00:02:20.369
one day. Ban studied international relations at Seoul National University, and in 1970 he began working for the Korean

18
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:31.675
Ministry of Foreign Affairs. He served in several countries, including India, Austria, and the United States, becoming part of the

19
00:02:31.676 --> 00:02:42.591
Ministry's United Nations division in 1975. Ban earned a master's degree in public administration at Harvard University in 1985,

20
00:02:42.592 --> 00:02:51.340
and he continued to take on more responsibilities at the Ministry. He quickly earned a reputation as a polite

21
00:02:51.341 --> 00:02:57.480
and reserved leader, even writing apology notes to people who had not been promoted as quickly as he had!

22
00:02:57.481 --> 00:03:06.490
In 2001 Ban's job at the Ministry ended abruptly when a diplomatic problem erupted. His staff had accidentally released

23
00:03:06.491 --> 00:03:14.466
a memo praising an antiballistic missile treaty that American President Bush did not support. Because the U.S. was a

24
00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:18.798
close and powerful ally, Korean President Kim Dae Jung was forced to make a public apology. BEN: That sounds

25
00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:25.558
like a big deal. Did it end Ban's career? OLIVIA: No, luckily it didn't end his career. But it was

26
00:03:25.559 --> 00:03:30.381
a very discouraging time for Ban. He was out of work and uncertain of what the future would hold.

27
00:03:30.382 --> 00:03:38.143
Eventually the president of the 2001 United Nations General Assembly offered Ban a job as chief of staff. To

28
00:03:38.144 --> 00:03:46.127
Ban, this new job offer seemed like very good luck, something he called a " jeon-hwa-we-bok experience." That's a

29
00:03:46.128 --> 00:03:50.696
Korean saying for a misfortune that turns into a blessing. Two years later Ban was appointed South Korea's foreign

30
00:03:50.697 --> 00:04:02.867
minister, and he was soon working out trade deals with other countries and leading talks aimed at stopping North

31
00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:08.771
Korea's nuclear weapons program. In 2006 Ban decided to run for secretary-general of the United Nations. The secretary-general is

32
00:04:08.772 --> 00:04:17.938
the head of the United Nations, presiding over meetings and traveling around the globe to meet with leaders. The

33
00:04:17.939 --> 00:04:29.006
secretary-general must act as an impartial moderator, but the position also involves informing the General Assembly of situations that threaten

34
00:04:29.007 --> 00:04:37.077
world peace or stability. This can lead to serious disagreements, so the secretary-general needs to be very careful when

35
00:04:37.078 --> 00:04:48.972
dealing with controversial issues. While Ban was campaigning for secretary-general, some critics questioned his leadership abilities, believing he was

36
00:04:48.973 --> 00:04:53.389
too quiet and polite to manage a job that was filled with so much controversy. Others, however, admired Ban's

37
00:04:53.390 --> 00:05:02.494
modest approach to his work. When Ban won the election, he actually addressed these concerns, saying modesty ". .

38
00:05:02.495 --> 00:05:09.390
. does not mean the lack of commitment or leadership. Rather it is quiet determination in action to get

39
00:05:09.391 --> 00:05:12.873
things done without so much fanfare." BEN: What has Ban done since becoming secretary-general? OLIVIA: Well, Ban has focused

40
00:05:12.874 --> 00:05:17.128
on many important issues. He's gotten involved in the United Nations' humanitarian response to disasters such as the earthquake in

41
00:05:17.129 --> 00:05:30.451
Haiti in 2010 and the accident at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant in 2011. He created UN Women,

42
00:05:30.452 --> 00:05:42.847
an agency that advocates for women's rights around the world, and he has spotlighted issues like global climate change,

43
00:05:42.848 --> 00:05:51.442
extreme poverty, and HIV/AIDS. He often says that his priority is to build bridges between nations and give voice

44
00:05:51.443 --> 00:06:05.084
to the world's most vulnerable and poor people. So the United Nations has also gotten involved in some perilous

45
00:06:05.085 --> 00:06:16.168
situations such as North Korea's testing of nuclear weapons and political unrest in places like Libya, the Ivory Coast,

46
00:06:16.169 --> 00:06:22.079
and Syria. As the leader of the United Nations, Ban constantly finds himself in an uncomfortable position; he's often

47
00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:28.578
a target for peoples' heated emotions and their sharp criticism. Still, his modest, steady approach to solving problems seems to

48
00:06:28.579 --> 00:06:34.314
have paid off, Ben. In 2011 Ban was unanimously elected for a second term as secretary-general. BEN: Wow, it

49
00:06:34.315 --> 00:06:38.477
sounds like Ban is involved in some pretty challenging and sensitive issues, Olivia. No wonder the job of secretary-general

50
00:06:38.478 --> 00:06:45.985
has been called impossible! We'll see you next week, Little Fox readers, to meet another person in the news.

